lessons.example:
SELECT c.customer_id, COUNT(p.payment_id) AS payments
FROM customer c LEFT JOIN payment p USING (customer_id)
GROUP BY c.customer_id;

WITH monthly AS (
 SELECT date_trunc('month', payment_date) AS month, SUM(amount) revenue
 FROM payment GROUP BY 1
)
SELECT *, LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) previous_revenue FROM monthly;

WITH revenue AS (
 SELECT i.film_id, SUM(p.amount) revenue
 FROM payment p JOIN rental r USING (rental_id)
 JOIN inventory i USING (inventory_id) GROUP BY i.film_id
) SELECT * FROM revenue;

exercises.solution:
Examples include:
- `film.original_language_id` may be null;
- `address.address2` and `address.postal_code` may be empty or null;
- `rental.return_date` may be null for an unreturned rental;
- a film may have no inventory;
- a customer could have no payments or rentals.
An inner join retains only matched rows. Use a left join when the business question requires retaining the entity on the left despite a missing optional relationship.

